/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   ClearSky-OMEGA · Site Finder circuit-claim ledger rule
   Paste inside the existing `match /databases/{database}/documents { ... }`
   block in firestore.rules, then DEPLOY.

     firebase deploy --only firestore:rules

   This backs the `sitefinder` tool. It is the FIRST tenant repo to ship it,
   because CIR is the first trial that unlocks Site Finder.

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WHY THIS ONE IS EASY TO MISS
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Site Finder degrades honestly without Firestore — the chip in the corner
   reads "Local only" and claims are held in the browser. That is the failure
   mode to worry about: a missing rule does not look broken. It looks like a
   working tool that quietly is not sharing claims between reps, which is the
   single thing the tool exists to do. Two reps will each hold the same
   circuit, both will size a battery against it, and nobody finds out until a
   site host agreement is already moving.

   So: after deploying, sign in as two different users in the same org, claim
   a circuit as one, and confirm it disappears from the other's inventory.
   The chip must NOT say "Local only". Run check-rules.js first.

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   DOC SHAPE
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Claims live at  capacityAllocations/{orgId}__{allocId}  and carry an orgId
   FIELD as well, deliberately. This DEVIATES from the toolData contract that
   sitefinder's `savesData: true` would otherwise imply, for the same reason
   `intake` does: toolData/{orgId}/tools/{key} is a single document and cannot
   be queried per-circuit, and the ledger has to be readable org-wide by every
   rep.

   ⚠ FIELD VALIDATION IS DELIBERATELY LOOSE.
   The authoritative claim schema lives in omega-capacity-ledger.js on
   TOOL_HOST, which is not in this repo. Rules below validate the tenant
   boundary — which is the part that actually matters for security — and only
   those fields the ledger is known to write. They do NOT enumerate the whole
   document. Tighten this once you have the ledger open in front of you;
   guessing a required field here fails writes closed and sends Site Finder
   back to "Local only" for the whole org.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

match /capacityAllocations/{allocId} {

  /* The org boundary, asserted two ways.

     ownedByCaller() checks the orgId FIELD — that is the one the query
     filters on and the one that governs reads.

     idScopedToCaller() checks the doc-ID PREFIX. Defense in depth: a client
     that wrote a correct orgId field into a doc named under another tenant's
     prefix would otherwise sit in that tenant's ID space. split() takes a
     regex; '__' is literal in RE2 and no org domain contains it. */
  function ownedByCaller() {
    return resource.data.orgId == userOrg();
  }
  function incomingOwnedByCaller() {
    return request.resource.data.orgId == userOrg();
  }
  function idScopedToCaller() {
    return allocId.split('__')[0] == userOrg();
  }

  /* Every rep in the org reads every claim in the org. That is the whole
     point — one rep's hold has to leave every other rep's inventory.

     NOTE: scope is per-TENANT, not platform-wide. Two different tenants can
     each hold the same ComEd circuit and neither will see the other. That is
     the correct default for a multi-tenant platform (CIR must not be able to
     enumerate another customer's pipeline), but it means the anti-double-sell
     guarantee is an INTERNAL one. Say so if a customer asks what the hold
     actually reserves — it reserves nothing with ComEd. */
  allow read: if request.auth != null && ownedByCaller();

  /* Claiming a circuit. Pinned to the caller's own org on both the field and
     the doc ID, and to the caller's own identity as the claimant, so a hold
     can't be planted under a colleague's name. */
  allow create: if request.auth != null
                && incomingOwnedByCaller()
                && idScopedToCaller()
                && request.resource.data.claimedBy == request.auth.token.email;

  /* Editing a live claim — resizing the hold, moving it through the funnel,
     releasing it. The org never changes hands and the doc never migrates to
     another tenant's ID space.

     Any rep in the org may edit any claim in the org, on purpose: reps cover
     for each other and a hold stranded on someone who left is worse than a
     hold someone else can release. If that turns out to be too open for a
     bigger tenant, add
         && resource.data.claimedBy == request.auth.token.email
     here and give admins their own path. */
  allow update: if request.auth != null
                && ownedByCaller()
                && incomingOwnedByCaller()
                && request.resource.data.orgId == resource.data.orgId
                && idScopedToCaller();

  /* Releasing a hold is a real operation, so unlike financeOffers this one is
     genuinely deletable. Prefer a status change where the ledger supports it
     — a deleted claim takes its history with it, and "who held this circuit
     in March and let it go" is a question that gets asked. */
  allow delete: if request.auth != null
                && ownedByCaller()
                && idScopedToCaller();
}
